                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A thing so unique we'll never get another like that. A game so unlike anything out there it was doomed to fail from the start. No flashy combat, no characters to get attached to, no dialogue, no handholding. You could say there's nothing here and you wouldn't be wrong. And yet I find it so great. The clunkyness of the combat works to the game's advantage, and it's actually beneficial to not just rush in but take your time or ignore enemies. Puzzles are somewhat cryptic at first but you get the hang of it very quickly. But it's not the gameplay that carries Scorn, it's the atmosphere, the visuals, the underlying themes of it all that you have to figure out for yourself or make them up in your head-canon. I am not easily impressed, but the game has stuck with me for a long time, especially the ending. Overall it's serviceable at best as a game, but it's an absolute masterpiece of interactive art. I've had games I poured hundreds of hours in and yet I remember so little of those. I remember EVERY second of Scorn and it will never leave my mind. In a world of endless sequels and remakes we need more fresh and brave ideas.",
